WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:07.440
Hello! In today's Bug Bounty Report Explained, you will&nbsp;
learn how you could have stolen private videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.760
from me or from other YouTube creators. The&nbsp;
vulnerability was reported by David Schutz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.280
and he got $5,000 for it. Links&nbsp;
to his blog post and to his twitter are in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:26.720
description. Enjoy! All videos on YouTube have&nbsp;
one of three different visibility attributes:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:34.160
public, which is self-explanatory, unlisted which&nbsp;
means that someone needs to have the link to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:41.840
video to watch it, or private which is also&nbsp;
quite obvious. And the goal of the bug hunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:49.600
was to leak the private video of another user. So&nbsp;
he created a second channel with a private video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:57.600
copied this video's id and on his first account&nbsp;
he replaced this id in all his requests looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:05.520
for insecure direct object references. But turned&nbsp;
out that all endpoints on YouTube were protected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:13.440
and error messages contained the information&nbsp;
"the video is private". However, Google has many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.000
products with which YouTube integrates. And the&nbsp;
good thing about integrations is that they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.680
often not as secure as the main product. So the&nbsp;
bug hunter started to look for vulnerabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:36.080
on Google Ads. It's a service that allows YouTube&nbsp;
creators to promote their videos or products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:44.080
For example, you can purchase an ad that will be&nbsp;
displayed before someone else's video and when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:50.400
customer clicks on the ad he gets redirected&nbsp;
to your video or to your external website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.600
But if I would create such advertisement I&nbsp;
wouldn't like to publish the 30 seconds video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:05.600
in your subscription feed, but I'd rather keep&nbsp;
it private. That's why Google Ads needs access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:14.000
to your private videos. And when creating an&nbsp;
advertisement you can select moments. For example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.240
you can define in which part of your video&nbsp;
company logo or the external link will appear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:28.400
And those moments are listed on the right hand&nbsp;
side along with the frame from the selected moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.480
This is the request that fetches that&nbsp;
thumbnail every time you create a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:43.200
Value of 1 is id of the video and value&nbsp;
of 2 is the moment in milliseconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.120
Before we continue I have a small announcement.&nbsp;
I've been getting messages that some of you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:58.080
not see my videos on YouTube even though&nbsp;
you are subscribed. That's why I created a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:05.120
mailing list to which you can sign up for free.&nbsp;
You will be notified about my new videos but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:14.000
also any bonuses or other materials that I may&nbsp;
create in the future. I promise privacy and no spam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.160
Link is in the description. Okay time&nbsp;
to come back to our vulnerability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:28.960
So the researcher replaced the video id with the&nbsp;
video from the second account as many times before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:36.960
However, this time it worked. The&nbsp;
response contained base64-encoded frame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.840
from the private video. So it was something,&nbsp;
but stealing one frame has relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:51.360
low impact. Let's see if it was possible to&nbsp;
steal the whole video. With 30 frames per second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.800
every next frame occurs roughly 33 milliseconds&nbsp;
later. The researcher created a proof of concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:08.480
that fetched all the frames from first 3 seconds&nbsp;
of the video. Then, he connected those frames into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:16.400
a GIF file to see the actual moving video.&nbsp;
This way you can steal whole private videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:23.040
Google paid $5,000 for this&nbsp;
report showing how important it is to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:31.920
However, the attack is a bit limited because you&nbsp;
need to know the video id and leaking this id&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:40.000
would itself be a vulnerability. Moreover, you&nbsp;
can't hear the video and also the resolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.040
is quite small. Of course, the vulnerability&nbsp;
has been parched and your private videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.920
are now secure. I think the main takeaway from this&nbsp;
report is that integrations are often less secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.760
than the main products and impact is still&nbsp;
equally high. Anyway, if you feel like you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.760
learned something new today leave a like and leave&nbsp;
a subscription. Thanks for watching and goodbye!

